# API Rules

## Responsibilities

- Define Server Actions as primary mutation/query API for internal use
- Standardize Route Handlers for external integrations only
- Enforce consistent error handling, validation, and response shapes

## Best Practices

- **Server Actions first** — `features/[feature]/actions/*.ts` with `"use server"`
- **Route Handlers** only for: webhooks, OAuth callbacks, file streaming, third-party APIs
- **Zod validation** at every entry point
- **Typed responses** — success/error discriminated union
- **Pagination** standard: `{ data, meta: { page, pageSize, total, hasMore } }`
- **Idempotency** for payment webhooks via idempotency keys
- Revalidate paths/tags after mutations

## Coding Standards

```typescript
// Standard action result type
type ActionResult<T> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: string; code?: string };

// Standard action structure
'use server';
export async function createCase(input: CreateCaseInput): Promise<ActionResult<Case>> {
  try {
    const session = await requireAuth();
    const parsed = createCaseSchema.parse(input);
    const caseData = await caseService.create(parsed, session);
    revalidatePath('/cases');
    return { success: true, data: caseData };
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof z.ZodError) {
      return { success: false, error: 'Invalid input', code: 'VALIDATION_ERROR' };
    }
    logger.error('createCase failed', { error });
    return { success: false, error: 'Failed to create case' };
  }
}
```

## Folder Structure

```
src/features/[feature]/actions/   # Feature Server Actions
src/actions/                      # Global actions (auth, profile)
src/app/api/                      # Route Handlers (webhooks only)
src/lib/api/                      # Response helpers, error codes
```

## Naming Convention

| Type | Pattern | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| Server Action file | kebab-case verb | `create-case.ts` |
| Action function | camelCase verb | `createCase`, `updateCaseStatus` |
| Route handler | REST noun path | `/api/webhooks/stripe` |
| Query action | get* / list* | `getCases`, `listInvoices` |

## Performance Rules

- Batch related reads in single action when needed by one view
- Cache tags for granular revalidation: `cases`, `case:${id}`
- Rate limit public Route Handlers

## Security Rules

- `requireAuth()` + `requirePermission()` at start of every action
- Validate organizationId ownership before update/delete
- Never return stack traces to client
- Webhook signature verification before processing
- CSRF: rely on Server Actions; for Route Handlers use signed tokens

## Examples

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — Route Handler for webhook only
// app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const body = await req.text();
  const sig = req.headers.get('stripe-signature')!;
  const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(body, sig, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET!);
  await paymentService.handleWebhook(event);
  return Response.json({ received: true });
}

// ❌ BAD — REST CRUD route for internal use
// app/api/cases/route.ts  → use Server Action instead
```

## Things to Avoid

- Exposing internal IDs without authorization checks
- Generic catch-all error messages without logging
- Returning Prisma models directly (map to DTOs)
- API routes duplicating Server Action logic
- Missing input validation

## Checklist Before Finishing Any Task

- [ ] Input validated with Zod schema
- [ ] Auth and permission checks present
- [ ] Returns typed ActionResult
- [ ] Errors logged server-side
- [ ] revalidatePath/Tag called after mutations
- [ ] No sensitive data in error responses
